% ============================================================================
% pond-zone — three named zones of a freshwater lake or pond and what each
% actually is (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A lake or pond is not one uniform body of water — ecologists sort it into
% named zones by where the water sits relative to the shore and to how deep
% sunlight reaches. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? pond_zone(littoral_zone, $D)
%       % close_to_the_shore
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `pond_zone(zone, description)` carrying the source citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a freshwater-zone term that is not one of these three. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the zone it
% names:
%
%     ? pond_zone($Z, deep_zone_located_below_the_range_of_effective_light_penetration)
%       % profundal_zone
%
% The three zones, as a little truth table:
%
%     zone              description                                                             quote (verbatim)
%     ----------------  ----------------------------------------------------------------------  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     littoral_zone      close_to_the_shore                                                       "The littoral zone, also called litoral or nearshore, is the part of an ocean, sea, lake, or river, that is close to the shore."
%     limnetic_zone      open_and_well_lit_area_of_a_freestanding_body_of_fresh_water              "The limnetic zone is the open and well-lit area of a freestanding body of fresh water, such as a lake or pond."
%     profundal_zone     deep_zone_located_below_the_range_of_effective_light_penetration          "The profundal zone is the deep zone of a lake, located below the range of effective light penetration."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from three separate Wikipedia articles ("Littoral
% zone", "Limnetic zone", "Profundal zone"), each article's own opening
% sentence. WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus` — the same
% tier this stdlib already reserves for other Wikipedia citations (e.g.
% `soil-texture-class.adj`'s element-to-periodic-table-group-family
% precedent in `chemistry/`). Because ADJ tables carry ONE shared provenance
% envelope, the envelope below cites the littoral-zone article at its
% `locator`, and each OTHER row's own distinct citation is documented above
% in prose (the same discipline `ocean-observing-instruments.adj`/
% `fable-moral.adj`/`rock-type.adj` already established for multi-source
% tables).
%
% Distinct from the already-shipped `oceanography/ocean-zones.adj`, which
% names three OCEAN depth zones (sunlight/twilight/midnight, ordered by how
% far sunlight reaches down through open ocean water) — a completely
% different body of water and a different organizing question (ocean depth
% vs. freshwater lake/pond position). Picked using the mandatory
% full-tree-grep-before-scoping discipline — `grep -rilE
% "\blittoral\b|\blimnetic\b|\bprofundal\b|\bpond_zone\b"
% code/specs/data/adj-facts-stdlib/` found ZERO hits before this file was
% written. A freshwater-zone term that is NOT one of these three — e.g. a
% deeper "benthic zone" some sources also name — is deliberately NOT a row
% here, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a description.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table pond_zone {
    columns zone, description

    row (littoral_zone, close_to_the_shore)
    row (limnetic_zone, open_and_well_lit_area_of_a_freestanding_body_of_fresh_water)
    row (profundal_zone, deep_zone_located_below_the_range_of_effective_light_penetration)

    source "The littoral zone, also called litoral or nearshore, is the part of an ocean, sea, lake, or river, that is close to the shore."
    locator "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Littoral_zone"
    trust consensus
}
